/* Pathology 13–14: Liver, gall bladder, pancreas, kidney, male genital tract */
Z("path13",0,"p",["Liver function tests are a panel of blood tests that show whether liver cells are damaged, bile flow is blocked, or the liver's making capacity has failed.","Think of the liver as a factory: enzymes leaking = broken walls (AST/ALT); blocked exit pipe = ALP/GGT/bilirubin; poor output = low albumin, long PT.",["Hepatocyte injury: ALT (more liver-specific), AST; AST:ALT > 2 in alcohol.","Cholestasis: ALP, GGT, conjugated bilirubin.","Synthetic function: albumin, prothrombin time (best acute marker).","Bilirubin: total, direct (conjugated), indirect (unconjugated)."],"LFT 3 groups: 'Leak (AST/ALT), Block (ALP/GGT), Make (albumin/PT)'."],
"Liver function tests are a group of biochemical tests reflecting hepatocyte integrity, biliary excretion and synthetic capacity. Markers of hepatocellular injury are aminotransferases: ALT (cytosolic, liver-specific) and AST (also in heart and muscle); very high levels (> 1000 IU/L) occur in viral, toxic and ischaemic hepatitis; AST:ALT > 2 suggests alcoholic liver disease. Markers of cholestasis are alkaline phosphatase (also bone, placenta), γ-glutamyl transferase (confirms hepatic origin, induced by alcohol) and 5'-nucleotidase, with raised conjugated bilirubin. Excretory function is assessed by serum bilirubin (total, direct, indirect) and urinary bilirubin and urobilinogen. Synthetic function: serum albumin (chronic disease) and prothrombin time/INR (acute, prognostic). Others: ammonia (encephalopathy), α-fetoprotein (HCC), viral markers.",
[["Classification of LFTs",[["Function","Test","Interpretation"],["Hepatocellular integrity","ALT, AST, LDH","↑ in hepatitis, necrosis"],["Cholestasis","ALP, GGT, 5'-NT","↑ in obstruction, infiltration"],["Excretion","Bilirubin (direct/indirect), urine bilirubin, urobilinogen","Type of jaundice"],["Synthesis","Albumin, PT/INR, cholesterol","↓ albumin, ↑ PT in liver failure"],["Detoxification","Blood ammonia","↑ in hepatic encephalopathy"],["Others","AFP, viral serology, autoantibodies","HCC, hepatitis, autoimmune"]]],
["Patterns",[["Pattern","AST/ALT","ALP","Bilirubin"],["Hepatocellular (hepatitis)","↑↑↑","N/↑","↑"],["Cholestatic (obstruction)","N/↑","↑↑↑","↑↑ (direct)"],["Haemolytic","N","N","↑ (indirect)"],["Cirrhosis","N/↑","N/↑","N/↑; ↓ albumin, ↑ PT"]]],
["Key points",["ALT is more specific for liver than AST","AST:ALT > 2: alcoholic liver disease","GGT confirms hepatic source of high ALP; raised in alcohol use","PT corrects with vitamin K in obstruction but not in hepatocellular failure","Albumin has half-life of 20 days — reflects chronic disease"]]],
{i:"No single test measures 'liver function'; LFTs are interpreted as patterns that point to hepatocellular, cholestatic or haemolytic processes.",x:[["Limitations",["Enzymes may be normal in compensated cirrhosis","Non-hepatic sources: AST (heart, muscle), ALP (bone, pregnancy)","Prothrombin time also affected by vitamin K deficiency and anticoagulants"]]],f:"Abnormal LFT\n ├ ↑↑ ALT/AST → hepatocellular (viral, drug, ischaemic, alcohol)\n ├ ↑↑ ALP + GGT → cholestasis → ultrasound (dilated ducts?)\n ├ ↑ indirect bilirubin only → haemolysis / Gilbert\n └ ↓ albumin, ↑ PT → synthetic failure (cirrhosis, acute liver failure)",c:"LFTs used as a panel and interpreted by pattern identify the type and severity of liver disease and guide further investigation."});

Z("path13",1,"p",["Jaundice is yellow colouring of skin and eyes due to excess bilirubin, from too much production, poor liver handling, or blocked bile flow.","Bilirubin is like garbage from old red cells: too much garbage (haemolysis), a broken processing plant (liver), or a blocked drain (obstruction) all make it pile up.",["Visible when serum bilirubin > 2–2.5 mg/dL; sclera first.","Prehepatic (haemolytic): unconjugated ↑, urine no bilirubin, urobilinogen ↑.","Hepatic: both fractions ↑, ↑ ALT/AST.","Posthepatic (obstructive): conjugated ↑, bilirubin in urine, pale stools, itching, ↑ ALP."],"Haemolytic = Acholuric; Obstructive = dark urine, Pale stool, Pruritus."],
"Jaundice (icterus) is yellow discoloration of skin, sclera and mucous membranes due to hyperbilirubinaemia, clinically evident when serum bilirubin exceeds 2–2.5 mg/dL (normal 0.3–1.2). Bilirubin is produced from haem (80% from senescent RBCs) by macrophages, transported bound to albumin as unconjugated bilirubin, taken up by hepatocytes, conjugated with glucuronic acid by UGT1A1, excreted into bile, converted to urobilinogen by gut bacteria, partly reabsorbed and excreted in urine, and the rest lost as stercobilin in faeces. Classification: prehepatic (haemolytic — excess production), hepatic (defective uptake, conjugation or excretion — hepatitis, cirrhosis, drugs, Gilbert, Crigler–Najjar, Dubin–Johnson) and posthepatic (obstructive — stones, carcinoma head of pancreas, strictures). Laboratory differentiation uses serum direct and indirect bilirubin, urine bilirubin and urobilinogen, stool colour, enzymes (ALT, ALP) and imaging.",
[["Bilirubin metabolism",["Haem → biliverdin (haem oxygenase) → bilirubin (biliverdin reductase) in macrophages","Unconjugated bilirubin: water-insoluble, albumin-bound, not filtered in urine","Hepatic uptake → conjugation by UGT1A1 → bilirubin diglucuronide","Excretion into bile (MRP2, rate-limiting) → gut → urobilinogen → stercobilin (stool) / urobilin (urine, 20% enterohepatic)"]],
["Classification",[["Type","Mechanism","Examples"],["Prehepatic","↑ bilirubin production","Haemolytic anaemias, ineffective erythropoiesis, large haematoma"],["Hepatic — unconjugated","↓ uptake/conjugation","Gilbert, Crigler–Najjar, physiological neonatal jaundice"],["Hepatic — mixed","Hepatocellular damage","Viral hepatitis, alcoholic, drugs, cirrhosis"],["Hepatic — conjugated","↓ excretion","Dubin–Johnson, Rotor, intrahepatic cholestasis"],["Posthepatic","Biliary obstruction","Gallstones, carcinoma head of pancreas, cholangiocarcinoma, stricture, biliary atresia"]]],
["Laboratory differentiation",[["Test","Haemolytic","Hepatocellular","Obstructive"],["Serum bilirubin","Unconjugated ↑","Both ↑","Conjugated ↑"],["Urine bilirubin","Absent (acholuric)","Present","Present (dark)"],["Urine urobilinogen","↑↑","↑ or N","Absent"],["Stool","Dark","Normal/pale","Clay-coloured"],["ALT/AST","Normal","↑↑↑","N/mild ↑"],["ALP","Normal","Mild ↑","↑↑↑"],["Other","Reticulocytosis, ↓ Hb","↑ PT, ↓ albumin","Pruritus, US: dilated ducts"]]]],
{i:"Jaundice is a sign, not a diagnosis; tracing the step at which bilirubin handling fails identifies the cause.",x:[["Hereditary hyperbilirubinaemias",[["Disorder","Defect","Bilirubin"],["Gilbert","Mild ↓ UGT1A1","Unconjugated, mild, fasting/stress"],["Crigler–Najjar I","Absent UGT1A1","Unconjugated, kernicterus, fatal"],["Crigler–Najjar II","↓ UGT1A1","Unconjugated, phenobarbitone responds"],["Dubin–Johnson","MRP2 defect","Conjugated, black liver"],["Rotor","Storage defect","Conjugated, normal liver colour"]]],["Neonatal jaundice",["Physiological (day 2–5): immature UGT, ↑ RBC turnover","Pathological: Rh/ABO haemolysis, G6PD, biliary atresia; kernicterus risk with unconjugated bilirubin"]]],f:"RBC breakdown → haem → unconjugated bilirubin (albumin-bound)\n → liver: uptake → conjugation (UGT1A1) → excretion into bile\n → gut: urobilinogen → stercobilin (stool) / urobilin (urine)\nPre-hepatic ↑ production | Hepatic ↓ uptake/conjugation/excretion | Post-hepatic blocked bile flow",c:"Serum bilirubin fractions with urine, stool and enzyme findings localise jaundice to a prehepatic, hepatic or posthepatic cause, and imaging confirms obstruction."});

Z("path13",2,"p",["Hepatitis B markers are blood tests for the virus's proteins and the body's antibodies; their pattern tells whether infection is acute, chronic, past, or vaccine immunity.","Antigens are the burglar's fingerprints (virus present); antibodies are the police record (immune response).",["HBsAg: infection present (first marker); > 6 months = chronic.","HBeAg & HBV DNA: active replication, high infectivity.","IgM anti-HBc: acute infection (also in 'window period').","Anti-HBs: immunity (recovery or vaccination)."],"'S' antigen = Sick now; anti-'S' = Safe; IgM core = Current; e = Extra infectious."],
"Hepatitis B virus is a partially double-stranded DNA hepadnavirus whose serological markers define stage of infection. HBsAg (surface antigen) appears first, 1–10 weeks after exposure, and persists beyond 6 months in chronic infection. HBeAg and HBV DNA indicate active viral replication and high infectivity. Anti-HBc IgM indicates acute infection and is the only marker in the window period, when HBsAg has disappeared and anti-HBs has not yet appeared; anti-HBc IgG indicates past or chronic infection. Anti-HBe indicates reduced replication (seroconversion). Anti-HBs indicates recovery and immunity; alone it indicates vaccination.",
[["Markers",[["Marker","Significance"],["HBsAg","Current infection; > 6 months = chronic"],["HBeAg","Active replication, high infectivity"],["HBV DNA","Viral load; best index of replication"],["IgM anti-HBc","Acute infection; window period"],["IgG anti-HBc","Past or chronic infection"],["Anti-HBe","Low replication"],["Anti-HBs","Immunity (recovered or vaccinated)"]]],
["Interpretation",[["HBsAg","Anti-HBc","Anti-HBs","Interpretation"],["+","IgM +","−","Acute hepatitis B"],["−","IgM +","−","Window period"],["+","IgG +","−","Chronic hepatitis B"],["−","IgG +","+","Resolved infection, immune"],["−","−","+","Vaccinated"]]]],
{c:"Interpreting HBsAg, anti-HBc and anti-HBs together identifies the phase of HBV infection; HBeAg and HBV DNA show infectivity and guide antiviral therapy."});

Z("path13",3,"p",["Cirrhosis is the end-stage of chronic liver injury: the whole liver becomes scarred and lumpy, with regenerating nodules surrounded by fibrous bands.","Like a city repeatedly bombed and rebuilt without a plan — scattered rebuilt blocks (nodules) separated by rubble walls (fibrosis) and broken roads (vascular shunts).",["Causes in India: alcohol, hepatitis B and C, NAFLD; also autoimmune, Wilson, haemochromatosis, biliary.","Stellate (Ito) cells become myofibroblasts and lay down collagen.","Micronodular (< 3 mm, alcohol) vs macronodular (> 3 mm, viral).","Consequences: portal hypertension, liver failure, HCC."],"Cirrhosis = 'Fibrosis + Nodules + Whole liver'."],
"Cirrhosis is a diffuse process of the liver in which normal architecture is replaced by regenerative parenchymal nodules surrounded by dense fibrous bands (bridging fibrosis), with vascular disorganisation. Causes: chronic alcohol use, chronic viral hepatitis B and C, non-alcoholic steatohepatitis, autoimmune hepatitis, biliary disease (primary biliary cholangitis, PSC, secondary biliary obstruction), metabolic diseases (haemochromatosis, Wilson disease, α1-antitrypsin deficiency), drugs and cardiac (congestive) causes; some are cryptogenic. Pathogenesis: chronic hepatocyte death, inflammation and activation of hepatic stellate cells into myofibroblasts that deposit type I and III collagen in the space of Disse (capillarisation of sinusoids), regenerative nodules and vascular shunts. Morphology: micronodular, macronodular or mixed. Clinical and laboratory features stem from hepatocellular failure (jaundice, hypoalbuminaemia, coagulopathy, encephalopathy, hyperoestrogenism) and portal hypertension (ascites, varices, splenomegaly). Complications include HCC and hepatorenal syndrome.",
[["Etiological classification",[["Category","Examples"],["Alcoholic","Chronic alcohol abuse (micronodular)"],["Viral","Hepatitis B, C, D (macronodular)"],["Metabolic","NASH, haemochromatosis, Wilson disease, α1-antitrypsin deficiency, galactosaemia"],["Biliary","Primary biliary cholangitis, PSC, biliary atresia, chronic obstruction"],["Immune","Autoimmune hepatitis"],["Vascular","Chronic congestion (cardiac cirrhosis), Budd–Chiari"],["Drugs/toxins","Methotrexate, INH, amiodarone"],["Cryptogenic","Unknown (often burnt-out NASH)"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Chronic hepatocyte injury and death","Kupffer cells and inflammatory cells release TGF-β, PDGF, TNF","Hepatic stellate cells activated → myofibroblasts → collagen in space of Disse","Loss of sinusoidal fenestrations (capillarisation) → impaired exchange","Regenerating nodules without normal vascular connections","Bridging fibrous septa link portal tracts and central veins; intrahepatic shunts → portal hypertension"]],
["Morphological classification",[["Type","Nodule size","Cause"],["Micronodular","< 3 mm, uniform","Alcohol, haemochromatosis, biliary"],["Macronodular","> 3 mm, variable","Viral hepatitis, Wilson, α1-AT"],["Mixed","Both","Late stages"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["LFT: ↑ bilirubin, mild ↑ AST/ALT (AST > ALT in alcohol), ↑ ALP/GGT (biliary)","↓ Albumin, reversed A:G ratio, ↑ γ-globulins","↑ PT/INR not corrected by vitamin K","Pancytopenia (hypersplenism), anaemia","Viral markers, autoantibodies, ferritin, ceruloplasmin, α1-AT","AFP for HCC surveillance","Liver biopsy (gold standard), elastography (FibroScan), ultrasound: nodular shrunken liver"]]],
{i:"Cirrhosis is a leading cause of death worldwide; in India alcohol and viral hepatitis account for most cases.",x:[["Clinical features",["Hepatocellular failure: jaundice, ascites, oedema, bleeding, spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, testicular atrophy, hepatic encephalopathy, fetor hepaticus","Portal hypertension: ascites, splenomegaly, oesophageal varices, caput medusae, haemorrhoids"]],["Complications",["Variceal bleeding","Hepatic encephalopathy, hepatorenal syndrome, hepatopulmonary syndrome","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Hepatocellular carcinoma"]]],f:"Chronic injury (alcohol, HBV/HCV, NASH)\n → hepatocyte death + inflammation → TGF-β\n → stellate cells → myofibroblasts → collagen (bridging fibrosis)\n → regenerative nodules + vascular shunts\n → liver failure + portal hypertension + HCC",c:"Cirrhosis is the common end-point of many chronic liver diseases; removing the cause early may halt fibrosis, but advanced cirrhosis needs management of complications and transplantation."});

Z("path13",4,"p",["Portal hypertension is raised pressure in the portal vein, usually from cirrhosis, causing blood to find detours (varices) and fluid to leak (ascites).","Like a traffic jam in a city centre (liver) forcing traffic onto narrow side roads that swell and burst.",["Normal portal pressure 5–10 mmHg; > 12 mmHg causes varices.","Causes: prehepatic (portal vein thrombosis), intrahepatic (cirrhosis — commonest; schistosomiasis), posthepatic (Budd–Chiari, CHF).","Effects: ascites, portosystemic shunts (oesophageal varices, caput medusae, haemorrhoids), splenomegaly, encephalopathy.","Variceal bleeding is the main killer."],"Portal hypertension 'ASCites, Splenomegaly, Shunts'."],
"Portal hypertension is increased resistance to portal blood flow causing raised portal venous pressure (hepatic venous pressure gradient > 5 mmHg; clinically significant > 10). Causes: prehepatic (portal vein thrombosis, splenic vein thrombosis, compression), intrahepatic (cirrhosis — commonest; non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis, schistosomiasis, massive fatty change, sarcoidosis) and posthepatic (hepatic vein thrombosis — Budd–Chiari, severe right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, veno-occlusive disease). In cirrhosis, increased resistance arises from fibrosis, nodules, sinusoidal constriction by stellate cells and endothelin, and increased splanchnic flow from nitric oxide vasodilatation. Consequences: ascites, portosystemic shunts (oesophageal and gastric varices, haemorrhoids, caput medusae), congestive splenomegaly with hypersplenism, and hepatic encephalopathy.",
[["Causes",[["Site","Examples"],["Prehepatic","Portal vein thrombosis, extrahepatic portal vein obstruction"],["Intrahepatic","Cirrhosis, NCPF, schistosomiasis, sarcoidosis"],["Posthepatic","Budd–Chiari, right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis"]]],
["Consequences",["Ascites: sinusoidal hypertension, hypoalbuminaemia, RAAS activation, splanchnic vasodilatation","Portosystemic shunts: oesophageal varices (massive haematemesis), rectal varices, caput medusae","Splenomegaly → hypersplenism (pancytopenia)","Hepatic encephalopathy (ammonia bypasses liver)","Hepatorenal syndrome"]]],
{c:"Portal hypertension explains most life-threatening complications of cirrhosis; β-blockers, banding of varices and TIPS reduce bleeding risk."});

Z("path13",5,"p",["Hepatocellular carcinoma is the main primary liver cancer, usually developing in a cirrhotic liver, especially with hepatitis B or C.","A liver repeatedly injured and repaired accumulates copying errors in its cells until one becomes cancerous.",["Risk factors: HBV (most important worldwide), HCV, alcohol cirrhosis, aflatoxin B1 (p53 mutation), NASH, haemochromatosis.","Raised serum α-fetoprotein (AFP).","Tumour invades portal and hepatic veins; spreads to lungs.","Fibrolamellar variant: young, no cirrhosis, better prognosis."],"HCC risk 'ABC': Aflatoxin, HBV, Cirrhosis (HCV, alcohol)."],
"Hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common primary malignant tumour of the liver, arising from hepatocytes, usually in cirrhotic livers. Major risk factors: chronic HBV infection (viral DNA integration and HBx protein), HCV, alcoholic cirrhosis, aflatoxin B1 (produced by Aspergillus flavus on stored grain, causing TP53 codon 249 mutation), NAFLD/NASH, haemochromatosis, α1-antitrypsin deficiency and tyrosinaemia. Grossly HCC may be unifocal massive, multifocal nodular or diffusely infiltrative, pale or green (bile), with strong propensity for vascular invasion (portal vein, hepatic vein, IVC). Microscopically: trabecular thickened plates, pseudoglandular or solid patterns of cells resembling hepatocytes, bile production, Mallory bodies. Serum AFP is raised in 50–75%. Presentation: upper abdominal pain, mass, weight loss, decompensation of cirrhosis. The fibrolamellar variant occurs in young adults without cirrhosis and has a better prognosis.",
[["Etiology and pathogenesis",["HBV: integration, HBx transactivates growth genes, chronic inflammation","HCV, alcohol, NASH: cirrhosis with regeneration-associated mutations","Aflatoxin B1: TP53 mutation (G→T at codon 249)","Common mutations: TP53, CTNNB1 (β-catenin), TERT promoter","Precursors: dysplastic nodules (large/small cell change)"]],
["Morphology",["Gross: solitary large mass, multiple nodules or diffuse infiltration; soft, haemorrhagic, bile-stained","Vascular invasion common; metastasis to lungs, nodes, bones","Micro: well-differentiated trabecular (> 3 cells thick), pseudoacinar, solid; bile, Mallory–Denk bodies; IHC HepPar-1, glypican-3","Fibrolamellar: large eosinophilic cells in lamellar collagen bands"]],
["Diagnosis",["Raised AFP (> 400 ng/mL strongly suggestive)","Ultrasound/triple-phase CT: arterial enhancement, venous washout","Biopsy if imaging inconclusive","Surveillance: 6-monthly ultrasound ± AFP in cirrhotics"]]],
{c:"HCC is largely preventable by HBV vaccination, HCV treatment, limiting alcohol and avoiding aflatoxin-contaminated food; surveillance of cirrhotics allows early curable detection."});

Z("path13",6,"p",["Cholelithiasis is stones in the gall bladder; cholecystitis is inflammation of the gall bladder, usually caused by a stone blocking the cystic duct.","Bile is a delicate solution; when it has too much cholesterol or pigment and moves too slowly, crystals form like sugar in thick syrup.",["Cholesterol stones (commonest): 'female, fat, forty, fertile, fair', OCP, rapid weight loss.","Pigment stones: black (haemolysis, cirrhosis) and brown (infection, parasites).","Acute cholecystitis: stone impaction in cystic duct; RUQ pain, fever, Murphy sign.","Chronic cholecystitis: thick fibrotic wall, Rokitansky–Aschoff sinuses; association with gall bladder cancer."],"5 Fs: Female, Fat, Forty, Fertile, Fair."],
"Cholelithiasis is the presence of gallstones. Cholesterol stones (80% in the West) form when bile is supersaturated with cholesterol, combined with gall bladder hypomotility, accelerated nucleation and mucus hypersecretion; risk factors include female sex, obesity, pregnancy, oestrogens, age, rapid weight loss, ileal disease and genetic factors. They are pale yellow, round, hard and radiolucent. Pigment stones are black (sterile gall bladder bile; chronic haemolysis, cirrhosis) or brown (infected bile ducts; parasites, E. coli). Acute calculous cholecystitis results from obstruction of the gall bladder neck or cystic duct with chemical irritation and secondary infection: enlarged, tense, red gall bladder with fibrinous exudate, possibly empyema, gangrene and perforation. Chronic cholecystitis follows repeated attacks: thickened fibrotic wall, mononuclear inflammation and Rokitansky–Aschoff sinuses. Complications: choledocholithiasis with obstructive jaundice, cholangitis, pancreatitis, gallstone ileus and carcinoma of the gall bladder.",
[["Types of gallstones",[["Type","Cause","Features"],["Cholesterol","Supersaturation, hypomotility, nucleation","Yellow, round, radiolucent, solitary or few"],["Black pigment","Chronic haemolysis, cirrhosis","Small, multiple, black, radio-opaque (50%)"],["Brown pigment","Biliary infection, parasites","Soft, greasy, in bile ducts"],["Mixed","Commonest in India","Faceted, laminated"]]],
["Cholecystitis",[["Feature","Acute","Chronic"],["Cause","Stone impaction (90%)","Repeated mild attacks, stones"],["Gross","Distended, red, oedematous, fibrinous serosa","Contracted, thick fibrotic wall"],["Micro","Oedema, neutrophils, necrosis","Lymphocytes, fibrosis, Rokitansky–Aschoff sinuses"],["Complications","Empyema, gangrene, perforation","Porcelain gall bladder, carcinoma"]]],
["Complications of gallstones",["Acute and chronic cholecystitis, mucocele, empyema","Choledocholithiasis → obstructive jaundice, ascending cholangitis","Acute pancreatitis","Gallstone ileus (fistula into bowel)","Carcinoma of gall bladder (high incidence in north India)"]]],
{c:"Most gallstones are silent; symptomatic stones and their complications are treated by laparoscopic cholecystectomy."});

Z("path13",7,"p",["Wilson disease is copper overload; haemochromatosis is iron overload — both genetic, both damage liver and other organs.","Two warehouses that cannot export their goods: copper piles up in Wilson disease, iron in haemochromatosis.",["Wilson: ATP7B mutation, AR; ↓ ceruloplasmin; liver, brain (basal ganglia), Kayser–Fleischer rings.","Wilson treatment: penicillamine, zinc.","Haemochromatosis: HFE (C282Y) mutation, AR; ↓ hepcidin → ↑ iron absorption.","'Bronze diabetes': cirrhosis + diabetes + skin pigmentation; ↑ ferritin and transferrin saturation."],"Wilson = 'Copper in Cornea & Caudate'; Haemochromatosis = 'Bronze diabetes'."],
"Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by ATP7B mutations impairing biliary copper excretion and incorporation into ceruloplasmin. Copper accumulates in liver (acute or chronic hepatitis, fatty change, cirrhosis, fulminant failure), brain (basal ganglia — tremor, dystonia, psychiatric features) and cornea (Kayser–Fleischer rings in Descemet membrane), presenting at 5–40 years. Lab: low serum ceruloplasmin, raised urinary copper, raised hepatic copper. Treatment: chelators (penicillamine, trientine) and zinc. Hereditary haemochromatosis is an autosomal recessive iron overload disorder, usually HFE mutation (C282Y), with reduced hepcidin and excessive intestinal iron absorption. Iron (haemosiderin) deposits in liver (micronodular cirrhosis, HCC), pancreas (diabetes), heart (cardiomyopathy), skin (bronze pigmentation), joints and pituitary (hypogonadism). Lab: raised transferrin saturation (> 45%) and ferritin, Prussian blue-positive hepatocytes. Treatment: phlebotomy.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Wilson disease","Haemochromatosis"],["Metal","Copper","Iron"],["Gene","ATP7B (chr 13)","HFE (chr 6)"],["Age","Children, young adults","Men 40–60"],["Organs","Liver, brain, cornea","Liver, pancreas, heart, skin, gonads"],["Signs","Kayser–Fleischer rings, movement disorder","Bronze diabetes, cardiomyopathy"],["Key tests","↓ Ceruloplasmin, ↑ urine & liver copper","↑ Transferrin saturation, ↑ ferritin"],["Treatment","Penicillamine, zinc","Venesection, desferrioxamine"]]]],
{c:"Both are treatable inherited metal overload disorders; early diagnosis by simple biochemical tests prevents irreversible cirrhosis and organ damage."});

Z("path13",8,"p",["Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease is fat build-up in the liver in people who do not drink much alcohol, linked to obesity and diabetes.","The liver becomes a storehouse for surplus calories; when overloaded it gets inflamed (NASH) and scarred.",["Spectrum: steatosis → steatohepatitis (NASH) → fibrosis → cirrhosis → HCC.","Linked with metabolic syndrome: obesity, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, insulin resistance.","NASH histology: steatosis, ballooning, Mallory–Denk bodies, lobular inflammation.","Commonest cause of abnormal LFTs; now called MASLD."],"NAFLD 'two hits': insulin resistance (fat) + oxidative stress (inflammation)."],
"Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (now termed metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease) is hepatic steatosis (> 5% of hepatocytes) in individuals without significant alcohol use. It is strongly associated with obesity, type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia and insulin resistance. Spectrum: simple steatosis, non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (steatosis with hepatocyte ballooning, lobular inflammation, Mallory–Denk bodies and pericellular fibrosis), cirrhosis and HCC. Pathogenesis: insulin resistance increases lipolysis and free fatty acid delivery to the liver; lipotoxicity, oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction and cytokines cause hepatocyte injury and stellate cell activation. Most patients are asymptomatic with mildly raised ALT (ALT > AST). Treatment: weight loss, exercise, control of diabetes and lipids.",
[["Spectrum",["Steatosis: macrovesicular fat in hepatocytes","NASH: + ballooning, inflammation, Mallory–Denk bodies","Fibrosis: perisinusoidal 'chicken-wire' pattern","Cirrhosis and HCC"]],
["Pathogenesis",["Insulin resistance → ↑ FFA flux to liver","Lipotoxicity, ROS, ER stress, mitochondrial injury","Gut-derived endotoxins, adipokines (↓ adiponectin)","Stellate cell activation → fibrosis"]],
["NAFLD vs alcoholic liver disease",[["Feature","NAFLD","Alcoholic"],["Alcohol","< 20–30 g/day","Significant"],["AST:ALT","< 1","> 2"],["GGT","Mild ↑","Markedly ↑"],["Histology","Similar","Similar, more Mallory bodies, neutrophils"]]]],
{c:"NAFLD is now the commonest chronic liver disease; lifestyle modification can reverse early stages."});

Z("path13",9,"p",["A liver abscess is a pus-filled cavity in the liver: pyogenic (bacteria), amoebic (Entamoeba histolytica) or hydatid cyst (Echinococcus tapeworm).","Pyogenic: multiple small pockets of pus; amoebic: one big 'anchovy-sauce' cavity, usually in right lobe; hydatid: a water-filled cyst with daughter cysts.",["Pyogenic: biliary infection (E. coli, Klebsiella), portal pyaemia (appendicitis), multiple.","Amoebic: young men, right lobe, single, 'anchovy sauce' pus without neutrophils; trophozoites at the wall.","Hydatid: dogs → sheep/humans; laminated cyst wall, germinal layer, daughter cysts, hydatid sand.","Rupture of hydatid cyst → anaphylaxis; do not aspirate blindly."],"Amoebic = Anchovy sauce; Hydatid = Hydatid sand + daughter cysts."],
"Liver abscesses are localised collections of pus. Pyogenic abscesses arise from ascending cholangitis (stones, tumours — commonest), portal pyaemia (appendicitis, diverticulitis), arterial seeding (septicaemia), direct extension or trauma; organisms are E. coli, Klebsiella, Streptococcus milleri and anaerobes; abscesses are often multiple. Amoebic liver abscess, caused by Entamoeba histolytica reaching the liver via the portal vein, is usually single, in the right lobe, in young adult males; it contains chocolate-brown 'anchovy sauce' necrotic material with few neutrophils, and trophozoites at the periphery; complications include rupture into pleura, lung, pericardium or peritoneum. Hydatid cyst is caused by larvae of Echinococcus granulosus (dog is definitive host, sheep and humans intermediate); usually in the right lobe, it has an outer host fibrous layer, a laminated acellular membrane and an inner germinal layer producing brood capsules, scolices (hydatid sand) and daughter cysts. Rupture may cause anaphylaxis and dissemination.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Pyogenic","Amoebic","Hydatid"],["Cause","E. coli, Klebsiella","E. histolytica","Echinococcus granulosus"],["Route","Biliary, portal","Portal vein from colon","Ingestion of eggs from dog faeces"],["Number","Multiple","Single","Single/multiple cysts"],["Site","Both lobes","Right lobe","Right lobe"],["Contents","Pus with neutrophils","Anchovy sauce, no neutrophils","Clear fluid, hydatid sand, daughter cysts"],["Diagnosis","Culture of aspirate, blood culture","Serology, US; trophozoites at wall","US/CT (daughter cysts), serology, Casoni test (old)"],["Treatment","Drainage + antibiotics","Metronidazole","Albendazole, PAIR, surgery"]]]],
{c:"Clinical setting, imaging and the nature of the aspirate distinguish the three types of liver abscess, each needing a different treatment."});

Z("path13",10,"p",["Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas: acute pancreatitis is sudden self-digestion by its own enzymes; chronic pancreatitis is long-term scarring with loss of function.","Like a factory storing acid that leaks from its tanks and dissolves the factory itself.",["Main causes: gallstones and alcohol ('GET SMASHED' mnemonic).","Acute: severe epigastric pain radiating to back; ↑ serum amylase and lipase.","Acute morphology: fat necrosis (chalky white, saponification), haemorrhage.","Chronic: fibrosis, calcification, duct stones → malabsorption, diabetes."],"Causes 'GET SMASHED': Gallstones, Ethanol, Trauma, Steroids, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion, Hypercalcaemia/lipidaemia, ERCP, Drugs."],
"Acute pancreatitis is reversible inflammatory injury of the pancreas caused by premature activation of pancreatic enzymes (trypsin activates others) within the gland, with autodigestion. Causes: gallstones and alcohol (80%), hypertriglyceridaemia, hypercalcaemia, drugs, trauma/ERCP, infections (mumps), hereditary (PRSS1, SPINK1), scorpion venom. Morphology: interstitial oedema, fat necrosis (chalky white foci from saponification), proteolytic destruction of parenchyma and haemorrhage from vessel necrosis. Clinical: severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, vomiting, shock; Grey Turner and Cullen signs; raised serum amylase and lipase; hypocalcaemia; complications include ARDS, DIC, pseudocyst and abscess. Chronic pancreatitis is long-standing inflammation with irreversible fibrosis, loss of acini and duct dilatation with calculi; causes are chronic alcoholism, tropical calcific pancreatitis (south India), hereditary and autoimmune; features are recurrent pain, malabsorption, steatorrhoea, diabetes and calcification on X-ray.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Acute","Chronic"],["Nature","Reversible","Irreversible"],["Pathology","Oedema, fat necrosis, haemorrhage","Fibrosis, atrophy, ductal calculi"],["Main causes","Gallstones, alcohol","Alcohol, tropical calcific, hereditary"],["Lab","↑↑ Amylase, lipase","Often normal enzymes; ↓ faecal elastase"],["Complications","Shock, ARDS, DIC, pseudocyst, abscess, hypocalcaemia","Malabsorption, diabetes, pseudocyst, carcinoma"]]],
["Pathogenesis of acute pancreatitis",["Duct obstruction (stone) → ↑ pressure, oedema, enzyme leak","Acinar cell injury (alcohol, drugs) → intracellular trypsin activation","Defective intracellular transport of proenzymes","Activated trypsin → phospholipase (fat necrosis), elastase (vessel damage), kallikrein (shock)"]]],
{c:"Acute pancreatitis ranges from mild oedema to fatal necrosis; chronic pancreatitis produces permanent exocrine and endocrine insufficiency."});

Z("path13",11,"p",["Pancreatic carcinoma is an aggressive adenocarcinoma of the pancreatic ducts, usually found late with very poor survival.","A silent intruder in a hidden place: by the time it causes symptoms, it has usually spread.",["Risk factors: smoking (most important), chronic pancreatitis, diabetes, obesity, family history (BRCA2).","KRAS mutation in > 90%; also CDKN2A, TP53, SMAD4.","Head of pancreas (60%): painless progressive obstructive jaundice, palpable gall bladder (Courvoisier).","Migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau sign); CA 19-9 tumour marker."],"Pancreas head cancer: 'Painless jaundice + palpable gall bladder = Courvoisier'."],
"Pancreatic carcinoma (infiltrating ductal adenocarcinoma) is the third or fourth leading cause of cancer death in developed countries, with five-year survival below 10%. Risk factors: smoking, chronic pancreatitis, diabetes, obesity, high-fat diet and hereditary syndromes (BRCA2, Lynch, PALB2). Precursor: pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN). Molecular changes: KRAS (> 90%), CDKN2A/p16, TP53, SMAD4. Location: head (60%), body (15%), tail (5%). Tumours are hard, grey-white, poorly demarcated, highly invasive with perineural invasion and dense desmoplasia. Head tumours obstruct the common bile duct, causing painless progressive jaundice with distended palpable gall bladder (Courvoisier law); body and tail tumours present late with pain, weight loss and metastases. Trousseau sign (migratory thrombophlebitis) occurs. CA 19-9 is raised.",
[["Features",["Gross: firm, grey-white, infiltrative mass; desmoplasia","Micro: moderately to poorly differentiated glands in dense stroma; perineural and vascular invasion","Spread: early to nodes, liver, peritoneum"]],
["Clinical",["Head: obstructive jaundice, pruritus, Courvoisier gall bladder","Body/tail: epigastric pain radiating to back, weight loss","Trousseau syndrome, new-onset diabetes","CA 19-9 raised; CT/EUS-guided biopsy"]]],
{c:"Pancreatic adenocarcinoma is usually incurable at diagnosis; avoiding tobacco is the most effective preventive measure."});

Z("path14",0,"p",["Renal function tests measure how well the kidneys filter blood and handle water and salts: urine examination, blood urea and creatinine, GFR, and tubular function tests.","Kidneys are a water-treatment plant; tests check the filters (GFR), the waste levels left in the water (urea/creatinine) and the fine-tuning pumps (tubules).",["Urine routine: protein, sugar, blood, casts, specific gravity.","Serum creatinine and urea; creatinine clearance and eGFR (best overall index).","Tubular function: urine concentration/dilution tests, acidification test.","Normal GFR ~ 125 mL/min; creatinine rises only after ~50% loss."],"RFT = 'Urine look, Blood waste, GFR filter, Tubule tune'."],
"Renal function tests assess glomerular filtration, tubular function and the urinary findings of renal disease. They include: (1) urine examination — physical (volume, colour, specific gravity, osmolality), chemical (protein, glucose, ketones, blood, bilirubin) and microscopy (RBCs, WBCs, casts, crystals); (2) blood biochemistry — urea, creatinine, uric acid, electrolytes, bicarbonate; (3) glomerular function — clearance tests (creatinine clearance, inulin clearance — gold standard, eGFR from MDRD/CKD-EPI equations, cystatin C); (4) tubular function — urine concentration (water deprivation) and dilution tests, urinary acidification (ammonium chloride test), fractional excretion of sodium, tubular proteinuria (β2-microglobulin); and (5) imaging and renal biopsy.",
[["Classification of RFTs",[["Group","Tests"],["Urine analysis","Volume, specific gravity, protein, glucose, blood, microscopy for casts and cells"],["Blood tests","Urea, creatinine, uric acid, Na⁺, K⁺, HCO₃⁻, Ca²⁺, PO₄³⁻"],["Glomerular function","Creatinine clearance, inulin clearance, eGFR, cystatin C, radionuclide GFR"],["Tubular function","Concentration & dilution tests, acidification test, FENa, β2-microglobulin, aminoaciduria"],["Others","Ultrasound, IVP, renal biopsy"]]],
["Interpretation",["Serum creatinine (0.6–1.2 mg/dL) depends on muscle mass; rises only after ~50% GFR loss","Urea: affected by protein intake, GI bleeding, dehydration (BUN:creatinine > 20 in prerenal)","Creatinine clearance = (U × V)/P; normal ~ 120 mL/min","Proteinuria > 3.5 g/day = nephrotic range; ACR detects microalbuminuria (diabetes)"]],
["Urinary casts",[["Cast","Significance"],["Hyaline","Normal, dehydration"],["RBC casts","Glomerulonephritis"],["WBC casts","Pyelonephritis"],["Granular/muddy brown","Acute tubular necrosis"],["Fatty (oval fat bodies)","Nephrotic syndrome"],["Waxy, broad","Chronic renal failure"]]]],
{i:"The kidneys regulate fluid, electrolytes, acid–base balance and excretion of waste; renal function tests detect disease and track its progression.",x:[["Prerenal vs renal failure",[["Index","Prerenal","ATN"],["Urine osmolality","> 500","< 350"],["Urine Na⁺","< 20","> 40"],["FENa","< 1%","> 2%"],["BUN:creatinine","> 20","< 15"],["Sediment","Bland","Muddy brown casts"]]]],c:"No single test suffices; urine examination, serum creatinine and estimated GFR together give a practical assessment of renal function."});

Z("path14",1,"p",["Polycystic kidney disease is an inherited condition where both kidneys fill with many fluid-filled cysts that slowly replace normal tissue.","Like a bunch of grapes replacing the kidney — each grape a cyst expanding until little working kidney is left.",["Adult (ADPKD): PKD1 (polycystin-1, chr 16) / PKD2; bilateral huge kidneys, renal failure at 40–50.","Associations: liver cysts, berry aneurysms (subarachnoid haemorrhage), mitral valve prolapse, hypertension.","Childhood (ARPKD): PKHD1 (fibrocystin), radial cysts, congenital hepatic fibrosis.","Symptoms: flank pain, haematuria, hypertension, palpable kidneys."],"ADPKD = Adult, Dominant, Plus berry aneurysm; ARPKD = Recessive, Radial, liver fibrosis."],
"Autosomal dominant (adult) polycystic kidney disease is a hereditary disorder with multiple expanding cysts of both kidneys that ultimately destroy the parenchyma and cause renal failure. It is caused by mutations in PKD1 (polycystin-1, 85%) or PKD2 (polycystin-2), proteins of primary cilia that regulate tubular cell growth and fluid secretion. Kidneys are bilaterally enlarged (up to 4 kg), composed of cysts of various sizes containing clear, turbid or haemorrhagic fluid. Presents at 30–50 years with flank pain, haematuria, hypertension, urinary infection and progressive renal failure. Extrarenal: hepatic cysts, berry aneurysms, mitral valve prolapse, colonic diverticula. Autosomal recessive (childhood) PKD, due to PKHD1 (fibrocystin), presents perinatally with enlarged smooth kidneys with radially arranged cylindrical cysts, congenital hepatic fibrosis and Potter sequence.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","ADPKD","ARPKD"],["Gene","PKD1 / PKD2","PKHD1"],["Age","Adults 30–50","Infants, perinatal"],["Kidneys","Huge, lumpy, cysts all sizes","Enlarged, smooth, radial cylindrical cysts"],["Liver","Cysts","Congenital hepatic fibrosis"],["Other","Berry aneurysm, MVP","Potter sequence, pulmonary hypoplasia"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Polycystins in primary cilia sense flow, regulate Ca²⁺ signalling","Defect → ↑ cAMP, cell proliferation and fluid secretion","Cysts arise from any part of the nephron and compress normal tissue"]]],
{c:"ADPKD is the commonest inherited renal disease and a significant cause of end-stage renal disease; screening relatives with ultrasound allows early blood pressure control."});

Z("path14",2,"p",["Glomerular diseases damage the kidney's tiny filters (glomeruli); most are immune-mediated, causing blood (nephritic) or protein (nephrotic) leakage into urine.","Glomeruli are coffee filters: immune complexes clog them, inflame them or punch holes in them.",["Primary (kidney only: minimal change, membranous, FSGS, IgA) vs secondary (SLE, diabetes, amyloid).","Immune mechanisms: circulating immune complexes, in-situ immune complexes, anti-GBM antibodies, cell-mediated, complement.","Immunofluorescence: granular (immune complex) vs linear (anti-GBM).","Present as nephritic, nephrotic, RPGN, asymptomatic haematuria or chronic renal failure."],"IF pattern: Granular = complexes; Linear = anti-GBM (Goodpasture)."],
"Glomerular diseases are a major cause of chronic kidney disease. They may be primary glomerulopathies (minimal change disease, FSGS, membranous nephropathy, MPGN, IgA nephropathy, post-infectious GN, crescentic GN) or secondary to systemic diseases (SLE, diabetes, amyloidosis, vasculitis, HIV, hepatitis B/C). Clinical syndromes: nephritic syndrome (haematuria, mild proteinuria, oliguria, hypertension, azotaemia), nephrotic syndrome (proteinuria > 3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, hyperlipidaemia), rapidly progressive GN, asymptomatic haematuria/proteinuria and chronic GN. Most are immune-mediated: antibody-mediated injury by deposition of circulating immune complexes (subendothelial or mesangial — granular IF), in-situ immune complex formation against intrinsic (anti-GBM — linear IF; PLA2R in membranous) or planted antigens (subepithelial deposits), cell-mediated injury, complement activation, and podocyte injury. Mediators include complement C5a/MAC, neutrophils, macrophages, cytokines and growth factors leading to proliferation, crescents and sclerosis.",
[["Classification",[["Primary","Secondary (systemic)","Hereditary"],["Minimal change disease","Lupus nephritis","Alport syndrome"],["FSGS","Diabetic nephropathy","Thin basement membrane disease"],["Membranous nephropathy","Amyloidosis","Fabry disease"],["MPGN","Goodpasture, ANCA vasculitis",""],["IgA nephropathy","HSP, bacterial endocarditis",""],["Acute post-infectious GN","HIV, hepatitis B/C",""],["Crescentic (RPGN)","",""]]],
["Clinical syndromes",[["Syndrome","Features","Example"],["Nephritic","Haematuria (RBC casts), oliguria, hypertension, mild proteinuria","Post-streptococcal GN"],["Nephrotic","Proteinuria > 3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminaemia, oedema, hyperlipidaemia","Minimal change, membranous"],["RPGN","Nephritic + rapid renal failure, crescents","Goodpasture, ANCA vasculitis"],["Asymptomatic haematuria","Recurrent gross haematuria","IgA nephropathy"],["Chronic GN","Slowly progressive renal failure","End-stage of many GN"]]],
["Immune mechanisms",[["Mechanism","Location of deposits","IF pattern","Example"],["Circulating immune complexes","Subendothelial, mesangial","Granular","Lupus nephritis, MPGN, PSGN"],["In-situ: planted antigen","Subepithelial","Granular","PSGN (humps), membranous"],["In-situ: intrinsic antigen (PLA2R)","Subepithelial","Granular","Membranous nephropathy"],["Anti-GBM antibody","Along GBM","Linear","Goodpasture syndrome"],["Pauci-immune (ANCA)","None/few","Negative","GPA, microscopic polyangiitis"],["Podocyte injury","None","Negative","Minimal change, FSGS"]]]],
{i:"Glomeruli are specialised filters (fenestrated endothelium, GBM, podocytes with slit diaphragms); immune injury to any layer alters filtration and causes proteinuria, haematuria or loss of GFR.",x:[["Mediators of injury",["Complement: C5a recruits neutrophils, MAC (C5b-9) injures epithelium","Neutrophils and macrophages: proteases, ROS","Cytokines (IL-1, TNF), PDGF (mesangial proliferation), TGF-β (sclerosis)","Fibrin leaks into Bowman space → crescents"]],["Investigations",["Urine: proteinuria, RBC casts","Serum complement (↓ in PSGN, MPGN, lupus), ANA, ANCA, anti-GBM, ASO","Renal biopsy: LM, IF, EM"]]],f:"Immune complexes / anti-GBM / ANCA / podocyte injury\n → complement + neutrophils + macrophages\n → endothelial, GBM, podocyte damage\n ├ nephritic (inflammation, haematuria)\n └ nephrotic (podocyte effacement, proteinuria)\n → sclerosis → chronic kidney disease",c:"Glomerular diseases are classified by clinical syndrome, cause and immunopathology; renal biopsy with immunofluorescence and electron microscopy is the key to diagnosis and treatment."});

Z("path14",3,"p",["Nephritic syndrome is an inflamed filter that leaks blood; nephrotic syndrome is a filter with holes that leaks lots of protein.","Nephritic: an angry, swollen sieve with red stains (RBCs). Nephrotic: a quiet sieve with big holes letting the protein escape.",["Nephritic: haematuria with RBC casts, hypertension, oliguria, mild proteinuria (< 3.5 g).","Nephrotic: proteinuria > 3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminaemia, generalised oedema, hyperlipidaemia, lipiduria.","Nephritic example: post-streptococcal GN; nephrotic: minimal change (children), membranous (adults).","Nephrotic complications: infections, thrombosis (renal vein), hyperlipidaemia."],"Nephr-ITIC = Inflammation; Nephr-OTIC = prOTein."],
"Nephritic syndrome results from glomerular inflammation with proliferation and leukocyte infiltration, damaging capillary walls and allowing red cells into urine. It presents with haematuria (RBC casts, dysmorphic RBCs), mild-to-moderate proteinuria, oliguria with azotaemia, hypertension and periorbital oedema; examples are post-streptococcal GN, IgA nephropathy, lupus nephritis and RPGN. Nephrotic syndrome results from damage to the filtration barrier (podocyte injury) causing massive proteinuria (> 3.5 g/day in adults), hypoalbuminaemia (< 3 g/dL), generalised oedema, hyperlipidaemia and lipiduria (oval fat bodies); urine sediment is bland. Causes: minimal change disease (children), membranous nephropathy, FSGS, MPGN, diabetes, amyloidosis, SLE. Complications: infections, thrombosis, hyperlipidaemia and protein malnutrition.",
[["Differences",[["Feature","Nephritic","Nephrotic"],["Mechanism","Glomerular inflammation","Podocyte/barrier damage"],["Onset","Acute","Insidious"],["Proteinuria","< 3.5 g/day","> 3.5 g/day"],["Haematuria","Present, RBC casts","Absent/minimal"],["Oedema","Mild, periorbital","Massive, generalised (anasarca)"],["Hypertension","Present","Usually absent"],["Serum albumin","Normal or mildly ↓","Markedly ↓"],["Lipids","Normal","↑ (lipiduria, fatty casts)"],["Renal function","Oliguria, azotaemia","Usually preserved initially"],["Examples","PSGN, IgA, RPGN, lupus","Minimal change, membranous, FSGS, diabetic"]]],
["Causes of nephrotic syndrome by age",["Children: minimal change disease (80%)","Adults: membranous, FSGS (commonest in some populations), diabetic nephropathy, amyloid, lupus"]]],
{c:"Distinguishing nephritic from nephrotic presentation narrows the differential diagnosis and directs the investigation of glomerular disease."});

Z("path14",4,"p",["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is kidney inflammation 1–4 weeks after a strep throat or skin infection, caused by immune complexes trapped in glomeruli.","The immune system's 'trash' (antigen–antibody complexes) clogs the kidney filters after fighting a strep infection.",["Children; follows group A β-haemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis or impetigo (nephritogenic strains).","Nephritic syndrome: cola-coloured urine, periorbital oedema, hypertension.","LM: enlarged hypercellular glomeruli; EM: subepithelial 'humps'; IF: granular IgG, C3.","↑ ASO, ↓ C3; excellent prognosis in children."],"PSGN = 'Humps after strep, Low C3, Cola urine'."],
"Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is an immune complex disease occurring 1–4 weeks after infection of the pharynx or skin by nephritogenic strains of group A β-haemolytic streptococci (antigen SpeB). It usually affects children aged 6–10 years. Immune complexes deposit (or form in situ) in glomeruli, activating complement. Presentation: acute nephritic syndrome with smoky or cola-coloured urine, periorbital oedema, hypertension, oliguria and mild proteinuria. Lab: RBC casts, raised ASO titre, low serum C3. Light microscopy: diffusely enlarged hypercellular glomeruli with endocapillary proliferation and neutrophils; IF: granular IgG and C3 (starry sky); EM: subepithelial electron-dense humps. Over 95% of children recover completely; adults fare worse.",
[["Pathology",["LM: diffuse proliferative, hypercellular glomeruli, neutrophils, occluded capillaries","IF: granular IgG, IgM, C3 along GBM and mesangium","EM: subepithelial humps"]],
["Diagnosis",["Urine: haematuria, RBC casts, mild proteinuria","↑ ASO / anti-DNase B; ↓ C3 (normalises in 6–8 weeks)","↑ Urea, creatinine"]],
["Outcome","Children: complete recovery in > 95%; few develop RPGN or chronic GN. Adults: 60% recover."]],
{c:"PSGN is the classic nephritic syndrome of childhood; recognition through history, low C3 and raised ASO is usually enough, and prognosis is excellent."});

Z("path14",5,"p",["Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis is severe glomerular damage causing kidney failure within weeks to months, marked by crescents in most glomeruli.","Glomeruli are being crushed by a crescent-shaped scar forming inside Bowman's capsule — a medical emergency.",["Crescents in > 50% of glomeruli (proliferating parietal cells + macrophages + fibrin).","Type I: anti-GBM (Goodpasture) — linear IF.","Type II: immune complex (PSGN, lupus, IgA, HSP) — granular IF.","Type III: pauci-immune, ANCA-associated (GPA, MPA)."],"RPGN types '1-2-3': Linear (anti-GBM), Lumpy (immune complex), Lacking (pauci-immune ANCA)."],
"Rapidly progressive (crescentic) glomerulonephritis is a clinical syndrome of nephritic features with rapid, progressive loss of renal function (often oliguria) over weeks to months, associated with crescent formation in most glomeruli. Crescents are formed by proliferation of parietal epithelial cells and infiltration of monocytes/macrophages in Bowman space, triggered by fibrin leaking through ruptured capillaries; they later become fibrous. Three immunological types: type I anti-GBM disease (linear IgG and C3 on IF; may involve lungs — Goodpasture syndrome with antibodies to collagen IV α3 chain), type II immune complex (granular IF; post-infectious, lupus, IgA, HSP) and type III pauci-immune (little or no deposits; ANCA-associated — GPA c-ANCA/PR3, microscopic polyangiitis p-ANCA/MPO). Treatment: plasmapheresis (type I), steroids and cyclophosphamide/rituximab; prognosis depends on the percentage of crescents.",
[["Types",[["Type","Mechanism","IF","Examples","Serology"],["I","Anti-GBM antibodies","Linear IgG, C3","Goodpasture syndrome, renal-limited anti-GBM","Anti-GBM"],["II","Immune complex","Granular","PSGN, lupus, IgA, HSP","↓ C3, ANA, ASO"],["III","Pauci-immune","Scant","GPA, MPA, renal-limited vasculitis","ANCA (c-/p-)"]]],
["Morphology",["Enlarged pale kidneys with petechiae","Crescents in > 50% of glomeruli; ruptured GBM; fibrin","Later: fibrous crescents, global sclerosis","EM: GBM ruptures; deposits only in type II"]],
["Clinical",["Haematuria, RBC casts, proteinuria, hypertension, oedema","Rapid rise in creatinine, oliguria","Goodpasture: haemoptysis (pulmonary haemorrhage) + RPGN"]]],
{i:"RPGN is a nephrological emergency; urgent biopsy and serology distinguish the types and guide immunosuppression before irreversible scarring.",x:[["Treatment and prognosis",["Type I: plasmapheresis + steroids + cyclophosphamide","Type III: steroids + cyclophosphamide/rituximab","Prognosis: worse with > 80% crescents, fibrous crescents, oliguria at presentation"]]],f:"Severe glomerular injury → GBM rupture → fibrin in Bowman space\n → parietal cell proliferation + macrophages → cellular crescent\n → compression of tuft → fibrous crescent → renal failure",c:"Crescentic GN can destroy the kidneys within weeks; early diagnosis by biopsy and specific serology allows effective immunosuppression."});

Z("path14",6,"p",["Membranoproliferative GN is a pattern of glomerular injury where both the basement membrane is thickened and the glomerular cells proliferate, often with complement activation.","The filter's walls get thicker and split into two layers (tram-track), while the cells inside multiply.",["Type I: immune complex — hepatitis C, hepatitis B, SLE, chronic infections; subendothelial deposits.","C3 glomerulopathy (dense deposit disease, old type II): alternative complement pathway, C3 nephritic factor.","LM: lobular hypercellular glomeruli, 'tram-track' double-contoured GBM.","Presents as nephrotic or mixed nephritic–nephrotic; low C3; poor prognosis."],"MPGN = 'Mesangium Proliferates, GBM Neo-layers (tram track)'."],
"Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis is a pattern of glomerular injury characterised by GBM alterations, proliferation of glomerular cells (mainly mesangial) and leukocyte infiltration. Mesangial cells interpose into the capillary wall and lay down new basement membrane, producing the double-contour 'tram-track' appearance on silver stain, with a lobular accentuation of glomeruli. Type I MPGN (immune complex) shows subendothelial deposits with granular IgG and C3, and is secondary to chronic infections (hepatitis C with cryoglobulinaemia, hepatitis B, endocarditis, shunt nephritis), SLE and malignancies. C3 glomerulopathy (dense deposit disease — previously type II) results from alternative complement pathway dysregulation, often from C3 nephritic factor; ribbon-like dense deposits are seen within the GBM with C3 only on IF. Patients (children and young adults) present with nephrotic or mixed nephritic–nephrotic syndrome and hypocomplementaemia; the disease often progresses to renal failure and recurs in transplants.",
[["Types",[["Feature","MPGN type I (immune complex)","Dense deposit disease / C3 glomerulopathy"],["Mechanism","Immune complexes, classical pathway","Alternative pathway dysregulation (C3NeF)"],["Deposits (EM)","Subendothelial","Dense ribbon-like within GBM"],["IF","IgG + C3 (granular)","C3 only"],["Causes","HCV, HBV, SLE, endocarditis","Idiopathic, factor H defects"],["Complement","↓ C3, ↓ C4","↓ C3, normal C4"]]],
["Morphology",["Large hypercellular glomeruli with lobular appearance","GBM thickened and split (tram-track) — mesangial interposition","Proliferation of mesangial and endothelial cells, leukocytes"]],
["Clinical",["Nephrotic syndrome with haematuria in older children/young adults","Hypocomplementaemia","Slowly progressive; 50% end-stage renal disease in 10 years; recurs after transplant"]]],
{c:"MPGN is a morphological pattern with immune complex and complement-mediated causes; finding and treating the underlying infection or complement defect is essential."});

Z("path14",7,"p",["IgA nephropathy is the commonest glomerulonephritis worldwide, where IgA deposits in the mesangium cause recurrent blood in urine, often after a sore throat.","IgA antibodies meant for the mucosa end up stuck in the kidney's central scaffolding (mesangium).",["Young men; gross haematuria 1–2 days after upper respiratory or GI infection (synpharyngitic).","Abnormal (galactose-deficient) IgA1 → immune complexes in mesangium.","IF: mesangial IgA deposits (diagnostic).","Henoch–Schönlein purpura is its systemic form."],"IgA = 'Immediately after Angina (throat)' haematuria."],
"IgA nephropathy (Berger disease) is the most common type of primary glomerulonephritis worldwide, characterised by prominent IgA deposits in the mesangium. It affects children and young adults, mainly males, presenting with recurrent gross haematuria within 1–2 days of an upper respiratory, GI or urinary infection, or with persistent microscopic haematuria and mild proteinuria. Pathogenesis: abnormal glycosylation (galactose-deficient IgA1), formation of autoantibodies against it, and deposition of immune complexes in the mesangium, activating the alternative and lectin pathways. LM: mesangial widening and proliferation, sometimes segmental sclerosis or crescents. IF: dominant mesangial IgA with C3; EM: mesangial deposits. Serum IgA is raised in half; C3 normal. Slowly progressive; 25–50% develop chronic renal failure over 20 years. HSP is the systemic counterpart (purpura, arthritis, abdominal pain).",
[["Pathology",["LM: mesangial proliferation (may be normal), focal proliferative GN","IF: mesangial IgA ± C3 (hallmark)","EM: electron-dense mesangial deposits"]],
["Clinical",["Synpharyngitic haematuria (1–2 days after infection) vs PSGN (1–4 weeks)","Normal complement","Poor prognosis markers: heavy proteinuria, hypertension, raised creatinine, crescents"]]],
{c:"IgA nephropathy should be considered in any young adult with recurrent haematuria following mucosal infections; mesangial IgA on biopsy confirms it."});

Z("path14",8,"p",["Diabetic nephropathy is kidney damage due to long-standing diabetes; it is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease.","High sugar slowly 'caramelises' the kidney's filters, thickening them and finally scarring them.",["Earliest sign: microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day).","GBM thickening (earliest structural change) and mesangial expansion.","Nodular glomerulosclerosis — Kimmelstiel–Wilson nodules (pathognomonic).","Also hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent and efferent arterioles, papillary necrosis, pyelonephritis."],"Diabetic kidney = 'K-W nodules + Microalbuminuria first'."],
"Diabetic nephropathy is a major microvascular complication of diabetes and the leading cause of end-stage renal disease. Hyperglycaemia causes non-enzymatic glycation of proteins (AGEs), activation of protein kinase C, glomerular hyperfiltration and hypertension, and growth factor (TGF-β) release, resulting in GBM thickening and mesangial matrix accumulation. Lesions: capillary basement membrane thickening (earliest), diffuse mesangial sclerosis, nodular glomerulosclerosis (Kimmelstiel–Wilson nodules — PAS-positive acellular nodules at the periphery of glomeruli), hyalinosis of afferent and efferent arterioles, exudative lesions (fibrin caps, capsular drops), and associated pyelonephritis and papillary necrosis. Clinically: microalbuminuria → overt proteinuria → nephrotic syndrome → progressive renal failure, usually after 10–15 years.",
[["Morphology",["GBM thickening (earliest)","Diffuse mesangial sclerosis","Nodular glomerulosclerosis: Kimmelstiel–Wilson nodules","Hyaline arteriolosclerosis (afferent + efferent — specific)","Fibrin caps, capsular drops","Renal papillary necrosis, pyelonephritis"]],
["Clinical stages",["Hyperfiltration","Microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day) — reversible with control","Overt proteinuria, hypertension","Nephrotic syndrome, falling GFR","End-stage renal disease"]],
["Prevention","Tight glycaemic and BP control; ACE inhibitors/ARBs and SGLT2 inhibitors slow progression; annual albumin–creatinine ratio screening."]],
{c:"Diabetic nephropathy can be detected early by microalbuminuria; strict control of glucose and blood pressure is the main defence."});

Z("path14",9,"p",["Acute tubular necrosis is death of kidney tubular cells from lack of blood (ischaemia) or toxins, the commonest cause of acute kidney injury.","Tubular cells are hard-working pumps with high energy needs; when blood or oxygen fails, or toxins poison them, they die and fall off, blocking the tubes.",["Ischaemic: shock, haemorrhage, sepsis, burns → patchy necrosis, basement membrane breaks (tubulorrhexis).","Nephrotoxic: aminoglycosides, contrast, heavy metals, myoglobin/haemoglobin → proximal tubule necrosis, BM intact.","Muddy brown granular casts in urine.","Phases: initiation, maintenance (oliguria, hyperkalaemia), recovery (polyuria, hypokalaemia)."],"ATN phases 'I-M-R': Initiation, Maintenance (oliguric), Recovery (polyuric)."],
"Acute tubular necrosis is a clinicopathological entity of tubular epithelial cell damage leading to acute decrease in renal function, the commonest cause of acute kidney injury. Ischaemic ATN follows hypotension and shock (haemorrhage, sepsis, burns, dehydration, crush injury); nephrotoxic ATN follows drugs (aminoglycosides, amphotericin, cisplatin, radiocontrast), heavy metals (mercury, lead), organic solvents (ethylene glycol), myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis) and haemoglobin (mismatched transfusion). Pathogenesis: tubular injury (loss of polarity, detachment, cast formation causing obstruction and back-leak of filtrate) and disturbed blood flow (intrarenal vasoconstriction via endothelin, reduced NO). Morphology: ischaemic ATN — focal necrosis along the nephron with breaks in basement membrane (tubulorrhexis), eosinophilic casts (Tamm–Horsfall) in distal tubules; toxic ATN — extensive proximal tubule necrosis with intact BM. Course: initiation, maintenance (oliguria, fluid overload, hyperkalaemia, uraemia) and recovery (polyuria, electrolyte loss). Regeneration allows full recovery if the patient survives.",
[["Causes",[["Ischaemic","Nephrotoxic"],["Shock (septic, cardiogenic, hypovolaemic)","Aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, cisplatin"],["Severe haemorrhage, burns","Radiocontrast agents"],["Crush injuries, pancreatitis","Heavy metals (mercury, lead), ethylene glycol"],["Mismatched transfusion (haemoglobin)","Myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis), snake venom"]]],
["Morphology",[["Feature","Ischaemic","Nephrotoxic"],["Distribution","Patchy, all segments, esp. straight PCT and thick ascending limb","Diffuse, proximal convoluted tubule"],["Basement membrane","Breaks (tubulorrhexis)","Intact"],["Casts","Pigmented granular, Tamm–Horsfall","Similar"],["Recovery","Slower","Faster (BM intact)"]]],
["Clinical course",["Initiation (36 h): mild oliguria, rising creatinine","Maintenance (days–weeks): oliguria 40–400 mL/day, salt & water overload, hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis, uraemia","Recovery: diuresis, hypokalaemia, infections; tubules regenerate"]]],
{i:"ATN is reversible because tubular epithelium regenerates; survival depends on supportive care through the oliguric phase.",x:[["Lab findings",["Urine: muddy brown granular casts, tubular cells","FENa > 2%, urine Na⁺ > 40 mmol/L, urine osmolality < 350","↑ Creatinine, urea, potassium; metabolic acidosis"]]],f:"Ischaemia / nephrotoxin\n → tubular cell injury (loss of polarity, detachment)\n → casts → obstruction + back-leak; vasoconstriction ↓ GFR\n → oliguria → regeneration → polyuric recovery",c:"ATN causes potentially reversible acute kidney injury; preventing hypotension and nephrotoxin exposure and supporting the patient through oliguria (dialysis if needed) allow full recovery."});

Z("path14",10,"p",["Nephrolithiasis is stone formation in the kidney from crystallisation of urine salts, causing severe colicky pain and blood in urine.","Like sugar crystallising in a supersaturated syrup when water is too little.",["Calcium oxalate stones are commonest (~70–80%).","Struvite (Mg-NH4-PO4): Proteus infection, staghorn calculi, alkaline urine.","Uric acid: gout, chemotherapy, acidic urine, radiolucent.","Cystine: inherited cystinuria, hexagonal crystals."],"Stones: 'Calcium Common, Struvite Staghorn (Proteus), Uric Under X-ray (radiolucent)'."],
"Nephrolithiasis is formation of stones (calculi) in the urinary tract, commonly in the kidney. Stones form when urine is supersaturated with poorly soluble salts, with low urine volume, altered pH and lack of inhibitors (citrate). Types: calcium oxalate/phosphate (70–80%; hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia, hyperparathyroidism), magnesium ammonium phosphate (struvite, 15%; urease-producing Proteus infection, alkaline urine, staghorn calculi), uric acid (5–10%; gout, high cell turnover, acidic urine; radiolucent) and cystine (cystinuria). Clinical: renal colic radiating from loin to groin, haematuria, infection, obstruction leading to hydronephrosis. Predisposing factors include dehydration, hot climate, diet and metabolic disorders.",
[["Types",[["Stone","Frequency","Cause","Urine pH","Features"],["Calcium oxalate/phosphate","70–80%","Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria","Variable","Radio-opaque, envelope crystals"],["Struvite","15%","Proteus (urease)","Alkaline","Staghorn calculus"],["Uric acid","5–10%","Gout, leukaemia therapy","Acid","Radiolucent"],["Cystine","1–2%","Cystinuria (AR)","Acid","Hexagonal crystals"]]],
["Complications",["Obstruction → hydronephrosis","Infection, pyonephrosis","Squamous metaplasia → carcinoma (rare)","Renal colic, haematuria"]]],
{c:"Identifying stone composition and the underlying metabolic cause guides treatment and prevention of recurrence; high fluid intake is the simplest preventive measure."});

Z("path14",11,"p",["Renal cell carcinoma is the main kidney cancer of adults, arising from tubular cells; the commonest type is clear cell, linked to VHL gene loss.","Clear cells are full of fat and glycogen that dissolves during processing, leaving them empty and clear.",["Risk: smoking (most important), obesity, hypertension, dialysis-associated cystic disease, von Hippel–Lindau.","Classic triad (late): haematuria, flank pain, palpable mass.","Paraneoplastic: polycythaemia (EPO), hypercalcaemia (PTHrP), hypertension (renin), Cushing.","Invades renal vein → IVC; 'cannonball' lung metastases."],"RCC triad 'Hurts, Haematuria, Has a mass'; VHL = 3p loss."],
"Renal cell carcinoma arises from renal tubular epithelium and accounts for 80–85% of adult renal cancers, peaking at 50–70 years with male predominance. Risk factors: smoking, obesity, hypertension, unopposed oestrogen, exposure to asbestos and cadmium, acquired cystic disease of dialysis, and hereditary syndromes (von Hippel–Lindau, hereditary papillary RCC). Types: clear cell (70–80%; VHL gene loss on chromosome 3p, HIF accumulation → VEGF), papillary (10–15%; MET, trisomies 7 and 17), chromophobe (5%; intercalated cells, excellent prognosis) and collecting duct types. Clear cell RCC is a solitary golden-yellow spherical mass at the poles with haemorrhage, necrosis and cysts, and a tendency to invade the renal vein. Microscopy: cells with clear cytoplasm (lipid, glycogen) in nests separated by delicate vasculature. Classic triad of haematuria, flank pain and mass occurs in only 10%. Paraneoplastic syndromes are common. Metastases: lungs (cannonball), bones, liver, adrenals.",
[["Types",[["Type","Frequency","Genetics","Features"],["Clear cell","70–80%","VHL loss (3p)","Golden yellow, clear cells, renal vein invasion"],["Papillary","10–15%","MET, trisomy 7, 17","Papillae with foamy macrophages, psammoma bodies; often multifocal"],["Chromophobe","5%","Multiple chromosome losses","Pale eosinophilic cells with perinuclear halo; good prognosis"],["Collecting duct","1%","—","Medulla, aggressive"]]],
["Morphology (clear cell)",["Gross: solitary, spherical, 3–15 cm, upper pole; bright yellow-orange with haemorrhage, necrosis, cysts","Tumour thrombus into renal vein and IVC","Micro: nests and sheets of clear cells, rich delicate vasculature; Fuhrman/ISUP nuclear grade"]],
["Clinical features",["Haematuria (commonest), flank pain, palpable mass","Fever, weight loss","Left varicocele (left renal vein obstruction)","Paraneoplastic: polycythaemia (EPO), hypercalcaemia (PTHrP), hypertension (renin), Cushing (ACTH), feminisation"]]],
{i:"RCC is called the 'internist's tumour' because it often presents with systemic and paraneoplastic features rather than urinary symptoms.",x:[["Spread and prognosis",["Haematogenous: lungs (cannonball), bone (lytic), liver, adrenal, brain","Prognosis: stage (vein invasion, perinephric fat), grade, type","Treatment: nephrectomy; targeted (VEGF/TKI, mTOR) and immunotherapy for metastases"]]],f:"VHL loss (3p) → HIF-1α accumulates\n → VEGF, PDGF, TGF-α → angiogenesis + proliferation\n → clear cell RCC → renal vein invasion → lung/bone metastases\n → paraneoplastic hormones (EPO, PTHrP, renin)",c:"Renal cell carcinoma is increasingly detected incidentally on imaging; molecular understanding of VHL–HIF signalling has led to effective targeted therapies."});

Z("path14",12,"p",["Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma) is the commonest kidney cancer of young children, made of embryonic kidney tissue.","A tumour that copies the developing kidney — blastema, tubules and connective tissue all mixed (triphasic).",["Age 2–5 years; large abdominal mass in a child.","Triphasic histology: blastemal, epithelial (tubules), stromal.","Genetics: WT1 (11p13), WT2; syndromes WAGR, Denys–Drash, Beckwith–Wiedemann.","Excellent cure rate (~90%); anaplasia = poor prognosis."],"Wilms = 'WAGR: Wilms, Aniridia, Genital anomalies, Retardation'."],
"Wilms tumour (nephroblastoma) is the most common primary renal tumour of childhood, usually diagnosed between 2 and 5 years. Associated syndromes: WAGR (Wilms tumour, aniridia, genital anomalies, mental retardation — deletion of 11p13 including WT1 and PAX6), Denys–Drash syndrome (WT1 point mutation, gonadal dysgenesis, nephropathy) and Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome (11p15, WT2, organomegaly, hemihypertrophy). Nephrogenic rests are precursor lesions. Grossly a large solitary, well-circumscribed, soft, tan-grey mass with haemorrhage and cysts; 10% bilateral. Microscopy: triphasic combination of blastemal cells (small blue cells), epithelial elements (abortive tubules and glomeruli) and stromal elements (fibroblastic, myxoid, sometimes skeletal muscle). Anaplasia (TP53) indicates poor prognosis. Presents with a large abdominal mass, haematuria, pain, hypertension. Combined surgery, chemotherapy and radiotherapy cure about 90%.",
[["Genetics and syndromes",[["Syndrome","Gene","Features"],["WAGR","WT1 deletion (11p13)","Aniridia, genital anomalies, retardation"],["Denys–Drash","WT1 point mutation","Gonadal dysgenesis, diffuse mesangial sclerosis"],["Beckwith–Wiedemann","WT2 (11p15), IGF2","Organomegaly, macroglossia, hemihypertrophy"]]],
["Morphology",["Gross: large, well-circumscribed, bulging tan-grey cut surface, haemorrhage, cysts, necrosis","Micro: triphasic — blastema, epithelium (tubules, glomeruloid), stroma","Anaplasia: large hyperchromatic pleomorphic nuclei, atypical mitoses → unfavourable"]],
["Wilms vs neuroblastoma",[["Feature","Wilms","Neuroblastoma"],["Origin","Kidney","Adrenal medulla/sympathetic chain"],["Midline crossing","Rarely","Often"],["Calcification","Uncommon","Common"],["Urine","Normal catecholamines","↑ VMA/HVA"]]]],
{c:"Wilms tumour is a model of a curable childhood cancer; multimodal therapy achieves cure in the large majority, while anaplasia identifies children needing intensified treatment."});

Z("path14",13,"p",["Hydronephrosis is swelling of the renal pelvis and calyces with urine because of obstruction to outflow, leading to kidney damage.","Like a blocked drain backing up water into the house, which slowly floods and damages the rooms (kidney tissue).",["Causes: stones, PUJ obstruction, strictures, tumours, BPH, pregnancy, posterior urethral valves.","Unilateral (above bladder) or bilateral (bladder outlet or below).","Progressive dilatation, thinning of cortex, atrophy.","Bilateral complete obstruction → acute renal failure; partial → polyuria (concentrating defect)."],"Hydro-nephrosis = 'water-kidney' from a block downstream."],
"Hydronephrosis is dilatation of the renal pelvis and calyces, with accompanying atrophy of the parenchyma, caused by obstruction to the outflow of urine. Causes: congenital (pelviureteric junction obstruction, posterior urethral valves, urethral atresia, vesicoureteral reflux), acquired (calculi, strictures, tumours of bladder, prostate, cervix, BPH, pregnancy, retroperitoneal fibrosis, neurogenic bladder). Obstruction raises pressure, glomerular filtration continues and urine diffuses into interstitium; compression of the vasculature causes ischaemia, tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis and finally a thin-walled cystic sac. Unilateral hydronephrosis may remain silent; bilateral partial obstruction causes polyuria and nocturia (concentrating defect), while complete bilateral obstruction leads to anuria and renal failure. Infection (pyonephrosis) and stones are complications.",
[["Causes",[["Level","Examples"],["Renal pelvis/ureter","PUJ obstruction, calculi, tumours, strictures, retroperitoneal fibrosis"],["Bladder","Tumour, neurogenic bladder, vesicoureteric reflux"],["Urethra","BPH, carcinoma prostate, posterior urethral valves, strictures"],["External","Pregnancy, cervical carcinoma, pelvic masses"]]],
["Morphology",["Early: pelvic and calyceal dilatation, flattening of papillae","Late: thin cortex, tubular atrophy, interstitial fibrosis, cystic sac","Infection → pyonephrosis"]]],
{c:"Early relief of obstruction can preserve renal function; long-standing obstruction causes irreversible atrophy."});

Z("path14",14,"p",["Testicular tumours are mostly germ cell tumours in young men; seminoma is the commonest and very radiosensitive.","Germ cells are the body's 'master seeds'; when they turn cancerous they can form any tissue (teratoma) or stay primitive (seminoma).",["Age 15–35; painless testicular swelling.","Risk: cryptorchidism (undescended testis), testicular dysgenesis, family history.","Seminoma: sheets of large clear cells with lymphocytic stroma; PLAP, OCT3/4 +; radiosensitive, excellent prognosis.","Non-seminoma: embryonal, yolk sac (AFP), choriocarcinoma (hCG), teratoma."],"Seminoma = 'Sensitive (radiation), Sheets, Stroma lymphocytes'."],
"Testicular tumours are the commonest solid malignancies in men aged 15–35. Over 95% are germ cell tumours, arising from germ cell neoplasia in situ (intratubular germ cell neoplasia); risk factors include cryptorchidism, testicular dysgenesis syndromes (Klinefelter, androgen insensitivity), family history and isochromosome 12p. They are divided into seminomatous and non-seminomatous germ cell tumours (embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumour, choriocarcinoma, teratoma, mixed). Seminoma is the most common: grossly a homogeneous grey-white lobulated mass without haemorrhage or necrosis; microscopically sheets of uniform large cells with clear cytoplasm, central nuclei and prominent nucleoli separated by fibrous septa containing lymphocytes, sometimes granulomas; PLAP, OCT3/4 and c-KIT positive. Seminomas spread to para-aortic nodes, are highly radiosensitive and have > 95% cure rates. Tumour markers: hCG (choriocarcinoma, some seminomas), AFP (yolk sac), LDH.",
[["Classification (WHO)",[["Group","Tumours"],["Germ cell (95%)","Seminoma; spermatocytic tumour; embryonal carcinoma; yolk sac tumour; choriocarcinoma; teratoma; mixed"],["Sex cord–stromal","Leydig cell tumour, Sertoli cell tumour"],["Others","Lymphoma (elderly men)"]]],
["Seminoma vs non-seminoma",[["Feature","Seminoma","NSGCT"],["Age","30s","20s"],["Gross","Homogeneous, grey-white","Variegated, haemorrhage, necrosis"],["Spread","Lymphatic","Blood and lymphatic"],["Markers","PLAP, OCT3/4; hCG in some","AFP, hCG"],["Radiosensitivity","High","Low"],["Prognosis","Excellent","Good with chemotherapy"]]],
["Tumour markers",["AFP: yolk sac tumour","β-hCG: choriocarcinoma, syncytiotrophoblast in seminoma","LDH: tumour burden"]]],
{c:"Testicular germ cell tumours are among the most curable solid cancers; orchidectomy with marker monitoring and radiotherapy or chemotherapy cures most patients."});

Z("path14",15,"p",["Benign prostatic hyperplasia is a non-cancerous enlargement of the prostate in older men that narrows the urethra and makes urination difficult.","Like a ring of doughnut swelling inward and squeezing the straw (urethra) that runs through it.",["Very common after 50; driven by dihydrotestosterone (DHT).","Arises in transition and periurethral zones (inner) — cancer mainly in peripheral zone.","Nodules of glands and stroma; corpora amylacea.","Symptoms: hesitancy, poor stream, frequency, nocturia, retention; complications hydronephrosis, UTI, stones."],"BPH = 'inner zone, DHT driven, not premalignant'."],
"Benign prostatic hyperplasia is a nodular hyperplasia of glands and stroma of the prostate, present in most men over 60. It is driven by dihydrotestosterone, formed from testosterone by 5α-reductase type 2 in stromal cells, which stimulates growth factors (FGF, TGF-β). It arises in the transition zone and periurethral glands (inner gland), compressing the prostatic urethra. Grossly the prostate is enlarged (60–100 g) with well-circumscribed nodules bulging from the cut surface, solid or cystic. Microscopically, nodules contain proliferating glands lined by two cell layers (inner columnar and outer basal) with papillary infoldings, corpora amylacea, and fibromuscular stroma. Clinical: lower urinary tract symptoms (hesitancy, intermittency, poor stream, dribbling, frequency, nocturia, urgency), acute retention, bladder hypertrophy with trabeculation and diverticula, hydronephrosis, UTI and stones. BPH is not premalignant. Treatment: α-blockers, 5α-reductase inhibitors (finasteride), TURP.",
[["BPH vs carcinoma prostate",[["Feature","BPH","Carcinoma"],["Zone","Transition/periurethral","Peripheral (posterior)"],["DRE","Smooth, firm, rubbery","Hard, irregular nodule"],["Glands","Two cell layers, papillary","Single layer, no basal cells, crowded small glands"],["Symptoms","Early urinary obstruction","Late; bone pain from metastases"],["PSA","Mild ↑","Marked ↑"],["Premalignant","No","—"]]],
["Complications",["Acute and chronic urinary retention","Bladder trabeculation, diverticula","Hydroureter, hydronephrosis, renal failure","UTI, bladder stones, haematuria"]]],
{c:"BPH is an extremely common age-related condition; it causes obstructive symptoms rather than malignancy, and responds to drugs or transurethral resection."});

Z("path14",16,"p",["Carcinoma prostate is cancer of the prostate glands, common in elderly men, usually in the peripheral zone, and spreads especially to bones.","A quiet cancer that often grows slowly at the back of the prostate and may show itself first through painful bone metastases.",["Most are adenocarcinomas in the peripheral zone; detected by DRE (hard nodule) and PSA.","Histology: small crowded glands, single cell layer (no basal cells), prominent nucleoli.","Gleason score (sum of two patterns, 2–10; now grade groups) predicts behaviour.","Osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone metastases in lumbar spine and pelvis."],"Prostate cancer: 'Peripheral, PSA, Painful bones (osteoBLASTIC)'."],
"Carcinoma of the prostate is the most common cancer in men in many countries, mostly after 50. Risk factors: age, race (African descent), family history (BRCA2, HOXB13), androgens (does not develop in castrated men) and diet. Molecular changes include TMPRSS2–ERG fusion, PTEN loss and AR amplification. 70% arise in the peripheral zone (palpable posteriorly on DRE). Precursor: high-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia. Microscopy: small crowded glands lined by a single layer of cuboidal/columnar cells, absent basal cell layer (p63 and HMWCK negative, AMACR positive), nuclear enlargement and prominent nucleoli, perineural invasion. Grading by Gleason system (primary + secondary pattern = score 6–10, ISUP grade groups 1–5). Spread: local (seminal vesicles, bladder base), lymphatic (obturator, iliac nodes) and haematogenous to bones (osteoblastic metastases to lumbar spine, pelvis, femur). Raised serum PSA; elevated ALP with bony metastases.",
[["Diagnosis",["DRE: hard irregular nodule","Serum PSA (> 4 ng/mL; PSA density, velocity, free/total ratio)","Multiparametric MRI, transrectal ultrasound-guided biopsy","Bone scan for metastases"]],
["Gleason grading",["Based on glandular architecture at low power","Patterns 3–5 used; score = most common + second/highest pattern","Grade group 1 (≤ 6) to grade group 5 (9–10)","Higher score → worse prognosis"]],
["Treatment",["Active surveillance for low grade","Radical prostatectomy, radiotherapy","Androgen deprivation therapy for advanced disease"]]],
{c:"Prostate carcinoma varies from indolent to lethal; PSA, biopsy and Gleason grade together decide whether to watch, treat locally or treat systemically."});

Z("path14",17,"p",["Urothelial carcinoma is cancer arising from the lining of the urinary bladder (and rest of the urinary tract), strongly linked to smoking and chemicals.","The bladder lining is constantly bathed in concentrated urine; carcinogens in that urine damage it over years.",["Risk factors: smoking (most important), aromatic amines (dye, rubber industry), cyclophosphamide, Schistosoma haematobium (squamous carcinoma).","Painless haematuria is the classic symptom.","Two pathways: papillary low-grade (frequent recurrence) and flat carcinoma in situ → invasive high-grade.","Prognosis depends on invasion of muscularis propria."],"Bladder cancer = 'Painless haematuria in a smoker'."],
"Urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma accounts for over 90% of bladder cancers, usually in men aged 50–80. Risk factors: cigarette smoking, occupational exposure to aromatic amines (β-naphthylamine — dye, rubber, leather industries), cyclophosphamide, phenacetin abuse, radiation; Schistosoma haematobium predisposes to squamous cell carcinoma. Two precursor pathways: non-invasive papillary tumours (FGFR3, HRAS; often low grade, recur frequently) and flat carcinoma in situ (TP53, RB; high grade, precursor of invasive cancer). WHO classification: papilloma, papillary urothelial neoplasm of low malignant potential, low-grade and high-grade papillary urothelial carcinoma, carcinoma in situ and invasive carcinoma. Presents with painless haematuria; frequency, urgency and dysuria with CIS. Diagnosis by cystoscopy and biopsy, urine cytology. Prognosis depends on grade and depth of invasion (muscularis propria).",
[["Classification",["Urothelial papilloma","PUNLMP","Papillary urothelial carcinoma — low/high grade","Urothelial carcinoma in situ (flat)","Invasive urothelial carcinoma; variants (squamous, glandular, micropapillary)","Other: squamous cell carcinoma (schistosomiasis), adenocarcinoma (urachal)"]],
["Features",["Gross: papillary, polypoid, nodular, flat or ulcerated; lateral walls and trigone","Micro: papillae lined by > 7 layers of atypical urothelium; high grade shows pleomorphism, mitoses","Spread: local to prostate, seminal vesicles, ureters; lymph nodes; late haematogenous"]]],
{c:"Urothelial carcinoma is common in smokers and industrial workers; cystoscopic surveillance is needed because of frequent recurrence."});

Z("path14",18,"p",["Chronic pyelonephritis is long-term scarring of the kidney from repeated infection and reflux or obstruction; it is an important cause of chronic renal failure.","Repeated invasions (infections) leave behind battle scars (coarse cortical scars) over the calyces.",["Two forms: reflux nephropathy (vesicoureteric reflux in children) and chronic obstructive pyelonephritis.","Coarse, irregular corticomedullary scars over blunted, deformed calyces (polar).","Histology: interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy with 'thyroidisation', chronic inflammation.","Chronic renal failure: anaemia, uraemia, hypertension, renal osteodystrophy."],"Chronic pyelonephritis = 'Calyx blunted + Coarse scars + Colloid-like tubules (thyroidisation)'."],
"Chronic pyelonephritis is a chronic tubulointerstitial inflammation and scarring involving the calyces and pelvis, caused by recurrent infections associated with vesicoureteric reflux (reflux nephropathy — commonest, begins in childhood) or chronic obstruction (stones, BPH, malformations). Grossly the kidneys are irregularly scarred, asymmetrical, with coarse, broad-based corticomedullary scars overlying dilated, blunted or deformed calyces, typically at the poles. Microscopy: uneven interstitial fibrosis with lymphocytes and plasma cells, dilated or atrophic tubules containing colloid casts (thyroidisation), periglomerular fibrosis, vascular sclerosis. It causes hypertension, proteinuria, impaired concentrating ability and progressive chronic renal failure. Chronic renal failure (chronic kidney disease) is the irreversible loss of nephrons from any cause (diabetes, hypertension, GN, chronic pyelonephritis, PKD), with uraemia, anaemia (↓ EPO), renal osteodystrophy, hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis, hypertension, pericarditis and bleeding tendency.",
[["Chronic pyelonephritis",["Reflux nephropathy: VUR + infection → polar scars","Obstructive: recurrent infection above obstruction","Gross: irregular asymmetrical scars, blunted calyces","Micro: patchy interstitial fibrosis, thyroidisation of tubules, chronic inflammation"]],
["Causes of chronic renal failure",["Diabetic nephropathy (commonest)","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Chronic glomerulonephritis","Chronic pyelonephritis/reflux","Polycystic kidney disease","Obstructive uropathy; drugs (analgesic nephropathy)"]],
["Effects of uraemia",[["System","Features"],["Blood","Normocytic anaemia (↓ EPO), bleeding (platelet dysfunction)"],["Bone","Renal osteodystrophy: secondary hyperparathyroidism, osteomalacia"],["CVS","Hypertension, uraemic pericarditis, heart failure"],["Metabolic","Hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis, hyperphosphataemia, hypocalcaemia"],["Others","Pruritus, encephalopathy, neuropathy, GI ulcers"]]]],
{c:"Chronic pyelonephritis is a preventable cause of renal failure; early detection of reflux and obstruction in children and effective treatment of infections preserve renal function."});
